<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Russell Clerk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir George Clerk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir George Russell Clerk was a British administrator who spent most of his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, twice serving as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>. In 1853–1854 he was special commissioner for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a>, sent to end British rule there, and on 23 February 1854 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein-convention">Bloemfontein Convention</a>, which recognised the independence of the Orange Free State.</p></div>
